{"id": "46075823-0d65-4ab0-9038-d6afafc2483d", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Found it: the EC2 interface endpoint has PrivateDnsEnabled set to false, which explains everything. Since Private DNS is disabled, DNS lookups for the EC2 API still resolve to public IPs, so traffic falls back to the NAT Gateway's default route instead of using the endpoint's private addresses.

The endpoint's subnets - subnet-001f766ca18453218 in us-east-1b and subnet-0cb5ed76f06a998b4 in us-east-1a - match exactly where Generator A and Generator B have their ENIs, so AZ coverage isn't the issue. I'll quickly verify the endpoint's security group sg-0fc85c69341d9c0a2 allows 443 from the instance SG sg-0c529bde2c2c8536c, and confirm the endpoint policy, just to rule out any secondary issues.

"}], "parent_id": "14aa1a73-52b1-49a1-916c-8031677f29ed"}

{"id": "a8e10588-235b-41c0-aacd-a7066f30c18b", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Security groups check out fine—the endpoint SG allows 443 from the VPC CIDR and instances have open egress, so that's not the blocker. Everything else looks correct too: subnets match the AZs, the policy is permissive, and the route table's NAT route is expected since interface endpoints don't rely on it. This confirms the real issue is PrivateDnsEnabled being set to false.

With Private DNS disabled, the standard EC2 API hostname resolves to a public IP rather than the endpoint's private IP, so traffic follows the default route to the NAT gateway instead of going directly to the ENI. I think this is enough to explain the NAT gateway traffic the user reported, and since this isn't a deep research request, I'll report the finding and suggest flow log verification as an optional next step rather than digging further myself.

